There is an application deployed on Kubernetes cluster. Recently, the Nautilus application development team developed a new version of the application that needs to be deployed now. 
As per new updates some new changes need to be made in this existing setup. So update the deployment and service as per details mentioned below:


We already have a deployment named nginx-deployment1-t2q3 and service named nginx-service1-t2q3. 
Some changes need to be made in this deployment and service, make sure not to delete the deployment and service.

Change the image from nginx:mainline-alpine3.18-slim to nginx:mainline-alpine-slim

Change NodePort to 32165

Note: The kubectl utility on jump-host has been configured to work with the kubernetes cluster.

deployment 'nginx-deployment1-t2q3' exists

Image 'nginx:mainline-alpine3.18-slim' is updated to 'nginx:mainline-alpine-slim'

Service 'nginx-service1-t2q3' exists

Pods are 'Running'

Website is not up and accessible

SOLUTION
Step 1: Inspect the current deployment and service
bash
kubectl get deployment nginx-deployment1-t2q3 -o yaml
kubectl get service nginx-service1-t2q3 -o yaml
Note the container name in the deployment (you'll need it for the kubectl set image command) and the current nodePort in the service.

Step 2: Update the image in the deployment
Use kubectl set image. The syntax is kubectl set image deployment/<name> <container-name>=<new-image>.

bash
kubectl set image deployment/nginx-deployment1-t2q3 <container-name>=nginx:mainline-alpine-slim
For example, if the container is named nginx:

bash
kubectl set image deployment/nginx-deployment1-t2q3 nginx=nginx:mainline-alpine-slim
Expected output:

text
deployment.apps/nginx-deployment1-t2q3 image updated
If you're unsure of the container name, retrieve it first:

bash
kubectl get deployment nginx-deployment1-t2q3 -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[*].name}'
Alternative: patch
bash
kubectl patch deployment nginx-deployment1-t2q3 --type=json \
  -p='[{"op":"replace","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/image","value":"nginx:mainline-alpine-slim"}]'
Step 3: Update the NodePort on the service
Use kubectl patch:

bash
kubectl patch service nginx-service1-t2q3 -p '{"spec":{"ports":[{"port":80,"targetPort":80,"nodePort":32165,"protocol":"TCP"}]}}'
⚠️ Important: The patch above replaces the entire ports array. If your service has a different port/targetPort/protocol, preserve those values — otherwise you can break the service. Safer: fetch the current ports first.

To inspect current ports:

bash
kubectl get service nginx-service1-t2q3 -o jsonpath='{.spec.ports}'
Then apply a targeted patch (using JSON Patch to replace only the nodePort at index 0):

bash
kubectl patch service nginx-service1-t2q3 --type=json \
  -p='[{"op":"replace","path":"/spec/ports/0/nodePort","value":32165}]'
Alternative: kubectl edit
bash
kubectl edit service nginx-service1-t2q3
Change nodePort: <old> → nodePort: 32165 and save.

Step 4: Verify everything
Deployment image:

bash
kubectl get deployment nginx-deployment1-t2q3 -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'
Expected:

text
nginx:mainline-alpine-slim
Rollout status (wait for pods to be updated):

bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx-deployment1-t2q3
Pods running:

bash
kubectl get pods
Expected: pods with nginx-deployment1-t2q3-xxxxx-xxxxx names in Running state.

Service NodePort:

bash
kubectl get service nginx-service1-t2q3
Expected output (NodePort column shows 32165):

text
NAME                    TYPE       CLUSTER-IP      EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)        AGE
nginx-service1-t2q3     NodePort   10.x.x.x        <none>        80:32165/TCP   ...
Website accessibility:

bash
curl -s http://<node-ip>:32165
Or, from the jump-host (which is often a node or can reach nodes):

bash
curl http://localhost:32165
