Notes / troubleshooting
Website not reachable after the change — check in this order:

kubectl get endpoints nginx-service1-t2q3 — the endpoints should list the pod IPs on the correct targetPort. If empty, the service selector doesn't match the pod labels.
Verify the service targetPort matches the container's containerPort (nginx listens on 80).
Verify the pods are actually Running and READY 1/1 — kubectl describe pod <pod>.
Test from inside the cluster: kubectl run tmp --image=curlimages/curl -it --rm -- curl http://nginx-service1-t2q3:80.
Check that port 32165 is not already in use by another service — kubectl get svc -A | grep 32165. NodePort range is 30000–32767 by default, so 32165 is valid.
Don't delete the deployment or service — all operations above are in-place updates, preserving them.

Rollout may take a moment — the old pod (using nginx:mainline-alpine3.18-slim) is replaced by a new pod (using nginx:mainline-alpine-slim) with a rolling update (default strategy). Use kubectl rollout status to wait.

If the set image command complains the container name doesn't exist, list container names again with the jsonpath above and retry with the correct name.

